<!--
  The bundled Node runtime's entitlements, and ONLY the bundled runtime's.

  ⚠ **This file is not the app's.** `entitlements.plist` beside it holds one
  entitlement and is meant to stay that way; these five would be a real loosening
  of the window that holds the fleet's credential. They belong to
  `Contents/Helpers/Reemoat Runtime.app`, the helper app the runtime lives in,
  which is a separate bundle with a separate signature and therefore a separate
  set.

  **Every key here was measured rather than chosen.** The official Node build from
  nodejs.org already ships signed with the hardened runtime, and this is the set it
  carries — read off `node-v24.21.0-darwin-arm64` by asking `codesign -d` for the
  entitlements of `bin/node` as XML, which answered `allow-jit`,
  `allow-unsigned-executable-memory`, `disable-executable-page-protection`,
  `disable-library-validation`, `allow-dyld-environment-variables` and
  `get-task-allow`. So this is not a guess about what V8 needs; it is what the
  people who build V8 ask for.

  ⚠ **No double hyphen appears anywhere in this comment, and that is not style.**
  XML forbids one inside a comment, and codesign's own parser refuses the whole
  file over it: `Failed to parse entitlements: AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error`.
  `plutil -lint` accepts the same file, which is how the command line quoted here
  verbatim went unnoticed for as long as nothing signed with this file: it could
  not have signed anything. `nativecheck` asserts the rule over every plist beside
  it.

  ⚠ **`com.apple.security.get-task-allow` is deliberately absent**, and it is the
  one key in that measurement that must never be copied. It lets another process
  attach a debugger and read this one's memory — which for the daemon means every
  transcript, the machine's signing keys and `identity.tunnel_key`. Node ships it
  because Node's own builds are debuggable; notarization rejects it outright, and
  that rejection is the only reason anybody would find it by accident rather than
  by reading this.

  **What each of the rest is for**, so a later reader can argue one away rather
  than assume all five are load-bearing:

    allow-jit                        V8 writes and then executes machine code.
                                     Without it the runtime does not start.
    allow-unsigned-executable-memory V8's older allocation path, still reached on
                                     some code paths; Node asks for both.
    disable-executable-page-protection
                                     the page-permission flips the JIT makes.
    disable-library-validation       Node `dlopen`s native addons, which are not
                                     signed by this project. The daemon ships
                                     none — `node:sqlite` is built in and the
                                     whole dependency set is pure JS — but a
                                     plugin is somebody else's code running as a
                                     child of this runtime, and `plugins.md` is
                                     explicit that the daemon does not control
                                     what it imports.
    allow-dyld-environment-variables
                                     Node reads DYLD_* for its own diagnostics.

  **Exercised on every build now, which it was not before.** Tauri's
  `entitlements` key names one file, for the app, and the bundler signs nothing in
  `bundle.macOS.files` — so the nested pass this file exists for is a step of its
  own: `build-daemon.mjs` signs the helper with it, under the hardened runtime,
  before the bundler seals the app around it. With `APPLE_SIGNING_IDENTITY` set
  that is the Developer ID signature notarization reads; with none it is ad-hoc,
  and the runtime still starts under the hardened runtime with exactly these five.
  So a development build is evidence for this file, where `docs/NATIVE.md` is right
  that it is still not evidence for the app's.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-executable-page-protection</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
